## Project at a glance

This project analyzes short student course feedback. It predicts whether a comment is positive, neutral, or negative, then groups comments into broad themes such as teaching, course materials, assessments, and pace. The goal is to help educators spot patterns in feedback faster. It is a decision-support demonstration, not an automated grading or student-evaluation system.

## Why this problem

Course teams receive many open-ended comments. Reading them one by one makes recurring praise and pain points harder to notice. A lightweight NLP workflow can label sentiment and surface frequently mentioned themes, while keeping the original comments available for human review.

## How it works

1. Load a CSV with `text` and `sentiment` columns.
2. Normalize text with a scikit-learn pipeline (lowercase, TF-IDF word features).
3. Train a Logistic Regression classifier.
4. Evaluate on a held-out split and report precision, recall, F1, and a confusion matrix.
5. Predict sentiment for new comments and assign transparent keyword-based themes.

The included CSV contains small, illustrative sample data only. It is not real student data and is too small to support claims about real-world accuracy. Replace it with a properly consented, anonymized dataset before drawing conclusions.

